#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060933 :tutorial/content "We can calculate the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ using the following five-step algorithm.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant $\\det(A)$ of the matrix.\n\n**Step 2:** Form the **matrix $M$ of the minors** of $A,$ where each entry $a_{ij}$ of $A$ is replaced by its minor $M_{ij}\\mathbin{:}$\n   $$\nM = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{M}_{11} & {M}_{12} & {M}_{13} \\\\ {M}_{21} & {M}_{22} & {M}_{23} \\\\ {M}_{31} & {M}_{32} & {M}_{33}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the **matrix $C$ of cofactors** by changing the sign of some entries of the matrix of the minors according to the **rule of alternating signs**:\n   \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}+ & - & + \\\\ - & + & - \\\\ + & - & +\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor each entry of $M$, we change the sign if the corresponding sign in the pattern is \"$-$\". Otherwise, if the corresponding sign in the pattern is \"$+$\", then we don't change the entry.\n\nSo, the matrix of cofactors is given by\n\n$$\nC = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{M}_{11} & - {M}_{12} & {M}_{13} \\\\ - {M}_{21} & {M}_{22} & - {M}_{23} \\\\ {M}_{31} & - {M}_{32} & {M}_{33}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors, $C^T.$\n\n$$\nC^T = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{M}_{11} & - {M}_{21} & {M}_{31} \\\\ - {M}_{12} & {M}_{22} & - {M}_{32} \\\\ {M}_{13} & - {M}_{23} & {M}_{33}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix:\n   \n\n$$\nA^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\det ( A)}C^{T}\n$$\n\nIn other words, we divide each entry of the transpose of the matrix of cofactors by the determinant of the original matrix (which we computed in step 1).\n\nLet's see this in practice, in the next example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9c7ba5cd-b4b2-595b-bbad-dfbdac248280" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3051 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060934 :tutorial/content "We make the matrix $C$ of cofactors by changing the sign of alternate entries of the matrix $M$ of the minors. So, each entry $c_{ij}$ of $C$ equals\n\n$$\nc_{ij} = (-1)^{i+j} M_{ij},\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the minor of the entry $a_{ij}$ in the matrix $A.$\n\nWith this and the formula for the inverse, we can write the formula for each entry $a'_{ij}$ of the inverse matrix $A^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\na'_{ij} = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}c^t_{ij}\n        = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)} c_{ji}\n$$\n\nHere, we used the fact that the entry $c^t_{ij}$ of $C^T$ is equal to the entry $c_{ji}$ of $C$ by the definition of the transpose." :tutorial/id #uuid "835c9ab0-dbce-5297-9a57-3978df178fa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3052 :tutorial/title "Calculating Individual Entries of the Matrix of Cofactors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088895 :tutorial/content "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A =\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nthe **inverse** of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9694c9ae-0d23-54a2-a458-b9c2262e4fba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3045 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088896 :tutorial/content "Remember that the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div> is given by\n\n$$\nA^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}\n\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $\\det(A)=0,$ then $A^{-1}$ doesn't exist since we can't divide by zero. In fact, we have the following statement:\n\n> $A^{-1}$ exists if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nA matrix that has an inverse is called **invertible**. Otherwise, the matrix doesn't have an inverse and is called **non-invertible**. A square matrix that is non-invertible is called **singular**." :tutorial/id #uuid "da6abb7f-408d-5c0d-bd11-70b1723463a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3046 :tutorial/title "Invertible and Singular Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088897 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A = \n\t\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe have been using the following formula to compute the inverse:\n\n$$\nA^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{ad-bc}\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo check that this formula is correct for a general matrix $A$, we need to verify that\n\n$$\nAA^{-1} = A^{-1}A = I_2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $A^{-1}A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}\\cdot A & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}ad-bc & 0 \\\\ 0 & -bc+ad\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $A A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}ad-bc & 0 \\\\ 0 & -bc+ad\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have verified that\n\n$$\nAA^{-1} = A^{-1}A = I_2,\n$$\n\nand we can be sure that the given formula is the correct formula for $A^{-1}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bb6e0c6d-f129-56b9-9bed-f9e6245197b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3047 :tutorial/title "Verifying the Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116490 :tutorial/content "Matrices can be used to represent systems of equations, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}2}x+{\\color{red}3}y &={\\color{blue}5} \\\\ {\\color{red}4}x\\text{ }{\\color{red}-}\\text{ }{\\color{red}5}y &={\\color{blue}-1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\times 2$ system like the one above, we put the coefficients of the system into a $2\\times 2$ matrix, called the **coefficient matrix**:\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}3} \\\\ {\\color{red}4} & {\\color{red}-5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEach row has the coefficients of one of the equations in the system, and each column consists of the coefficients of one particular variable.\nWe also have the $2\\times 1$ column vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}5} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-1}\\end{bmatrix} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b}$ consists of each constant on the right side of the system and $\\mathbf{x}$ contains the variables.\nWith these matrices, we can write our system as $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ or\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}3} \\\\ {\\color{red}4} & {\\color{red}-5}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}5} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can write any system of two equations with two variables in this form.\nJust to check that the above equation is true, let's multiply out the left-hand side to see if it equals the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}3} \\\\ {\\color{red}4} & {\\color{red}-5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}2}x+{\\color{red}3}y \\\\ {\\color{red}4}x\\text{ }{\\color{red}-}\\text{ }{\\color{red}5}y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}5} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =b\\,{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d49da49a-c3f8-5718-91a9-4adc236155d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3048 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121025 :tutorial/content "The minor of the entry $a_{ij}$ in a $n\\times n$ matrix is the determinant that we get when the rows and columns containing $a_{ij}$ are removed.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to find the minor of $a_{32}$ in the following matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 5 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nTo get the minor of $a_{\\color{red}{3}\\color{blue}{2}}$, we remove the $\\color{red}3$rd row and $\\color{blue}2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 5 & 12 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}-2} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ -5 & {\\color{gray}∗} & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of $a_{32}$, denoted $M_{32}$, is \n\n$$\nM_{32}=\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 12 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the value of $M_{32}$ by evaluating the determinant using previously discussed methods." :tutorial/id #uuid "c271b45c-6e89-58a5-b33f-72802abba7b7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3020 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121059 :tutorial/content "One way to find the determinant of a $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix $A$ is to take the alternating sum of the entries of the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}\n$$\n\nRemember that we had to change the sign of the second product. When we multiply a minor of an entry by the respective sign in the formula like this, we get the cofactor of the entry. The cofactors in the above formula were ${\\color{blue}M_{11}},$ $-{\\color{blue}M_{12}},$ and ${\\color{blue}M_{13}}.$\n\nUsing this idea, we can write the formula for the determinant of a $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix $A$ in terms of cofactors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}C_{11}} + {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}C_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}C_{13}}\n$$\n\nThis is called the **cofactor expansion** of the determinant across the first row.\n\nIn fact, we can use a similar method of multiplying the entries of the top row of the matrix by their respective cofactors to find the determinant for any $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$ when $n \\ge 3$. The formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{1j}{C}_{1j} \\\\  & ={a}_{11}{C}_{11}+{a}_{12}{C}_{12}+⋯+{a}_{1n}{C}_{1n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see how to use this formula in the next example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d83d34fa-1d87-5510-bfd8-8673b2bd79cf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3801 :tutorial/title "Calculating Determinants Using Cofactors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121060 :tutorial/content "In general, for any $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **cofactor** $C_{ij}$ of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}C_{ij}}=(-1)^{i+j}{\\color{blue}M_{ij}}.\n$$\n\nCofactors are a useful tool in helping us to compute determinants of larger matrices. Before we learn how, let's get some practice at computing cofactors." :tutorial/id #uuid "6c2b7774-d437-5cf9-812f-4d6ad8dc9bf3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3802 :tutorial/title "Calculating a Cofactor of a NxN Matrix"}]}
